															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/deadly-bread" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3988532); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3988532&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200992" alt="Deadly Bread" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3988532)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/deadly-bread" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3988532); return false;" >Deadly Bread</a></span> <span class="odate time_1539366836 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Oct 2018 17:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4022282">
									

<p>First of all, I like this concept. This is nice, especially for a first time scip. I’m just prefacing this with that so you know I don’t dislike the article. There are just some phrasing issues that I’d like to point out so they can be improved. Also, I’m not going to be covering the interviews because I’m admittedly not the best with actual dialogue so I wouldn’t be able to provide the best suggestions.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Here’s&nbsp;the&nbsp;crit&nbsp;yes</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Testing may be performed by Class-D Personnel or Level Three Site Personnel strictly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The use of both “may” and “strictly” in the same procedure makes it sound a bit off. I’d suggest just cutting out both from the sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following the use of SCP-4865, the person who used it must record what they obtained from the object before exiting the room</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would just simplify this down to “All objects produced by SCP-4865 are to be documented, along with the subject who retrieved the item.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The key lock on the door is broken, and no key will fit in the lock. This does not matter, as SCP-4865 is currently in an unlocked state.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence feels a bit unnecessary and could probably just be incorporated into the description by saying it has a broken lock.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The bottom of SCP-4865 is extremely corroded, yet has not lost any of its structural strength. The corrosion also has an effect on what SCP-4865 is placed upon, however it usually does not suffer effects from the corrosion.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is more of a personal nitpick, but the corrosion effect feels a bit tacked on. The SCP seems like it wants to be a gift giving safe with another works and religion on the other side, but “Hey also the bottom corrodes things.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If the door is opened without the requirements to trigger an anomalous result</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The wording is a bit weird here. I’d suggest just changing it to “When SCP-4865 is opened normally,”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is also unknown how SCP-4865 selects what to give the person who opens it. The reason for this is currently the subject of ongoing research.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think it would look better if this was merged with the section above.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There are nineteen objects obtained from SCP-4865 that have been publicly released for examination by researchers, post containment. These are labelled Product-A to Product-S.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don’t think you need to say the amount of items here because they could just be counted in the log. I’d rephrase the sentence as just, “A log of objects obtained from SCP-4865, which are to be referred to as Product-A through Product-S, has been included below.” Also, big of a personal preference here, but I’d suggest changing them from things like Product-A to SCP-4865-A.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>26th August</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’d put the month before the date.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One revolver, with bullets already loaded in the weapon.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Could probably change this to “One loaded revolver.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This confirmed SCP-4865 uses the currency of the UK despite not being located in the UK at the time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This should be reworded into something similar to, “This confirmed that currency SCP-4865 will be in pounds, despite not currently being located within the United Kingdom.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nobody knows why he wanted this, other than the fact that they were extremely sharp.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’d just remove this sentence entirely.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The researchers did not follow protocol and assumed this confectionery item was safe to consume, similar to Product-H. However, the consumption of this resulted in the death of two researchers, involving poisoning by chlorine gas. The remaining researcher was reprimanded following the incident.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To me, this seems too unprofessional of a member of the Foundation. If a cupcake was created by a magic safe covered in a weird white powder, I don’t think a scientist would just take a big old bite out of that sucker.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He successfully consumed it, however the researchers were able to resuscitate him every time his heart stopped until medical personnel arrived. Testing involving D-1034 was permanently suspended.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’d change this to, “Subject successfully consumed the arsenic, however researchers were able to stabilize subject until medical personnel arrived.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Doctor Square had lost his keycard at the time, and had been desperate to find it. Containment procedures were revised following this, to prevent lower level personnel from obtaining higher level access.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’d change this to, “Dr. Square had lost his keycard at the time, and was concerned about being repromanded for doing so. Following this, containment procedures were revised to prevent SCP-4865 from being used to find lost objects.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The fat man&quot; was later revealed to be an imaginary person, and D-8235 had previously unknown schizophrenia.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Probably change this to “undiagnosed schizophrenia” instead of “unknown schizophrenia”.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>researchers discovered it flew a lot farther than what was thought possible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I’d change it to “it flew much farther than expected.”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is unknown what this means, though the phrase &quot;take a shot&quot; presumably means with a camera; not a gun.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The explanation of the phrase “Take a shot” seems unnecessary</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Considering that D-1987 is responsible for the murder of █ women in the year 200█, it was presumed that this was one of his sickly ideas.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The formal tone really breaks here. Either overhaul this sentence or remove it entirely.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The arm was coated in what felt like a deep layer of rust, which eventually was grown over by skin during the days following the experiment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why wouldn’t they get a sample of this stuff instead of just saying it felt like rust? Also how was it grown over by skin?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following an unexpected incident in which a penny was dropped in to the stainless steel item's slot and it subsequently appeared in SCP-4865, a test has been designated involving the object, giving the object designation SCP-4865-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The penny is SCP-4865-1? How is it anomalous? What test involving it? This sentence is really confusing, as to why the penny is even anomalous, what test, what did the coin slot even do?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Again, I like this SCP. I just think it needs some tweaking and it would be great.</p>

